WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.734
Special Counsel

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:02.002
Jack Smith's investigation

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:03.403
into classified documents

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:04.571
at former presidents,

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:06.439
President Trump's Mar a Lago resort.

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:07.741
CNN has learned that a key

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:09.976
attorney is leaving Trump's legal team.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.511
Caitlyn, this is your reporting

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.645
first on CNN.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:13.380
What can you tell us?

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:13.847
Who is it?

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:15.749
Paul Harvey never told Tim Pawlenty,

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.083
who has been an attorney

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.218
at the center of the Mar

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:20.053
a Lago documents investigation.

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:21.588
Is leaving Trump's legal team.

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:23.323
It's been rumored for a few weeks now.

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:24.924
It became official yesterday.

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:25.592
We are told

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:26.593
he gave us a statement

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:27.761
saying that it was it,

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:28.995
that it was a personal decision

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:29.496
he's making

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:30.330
that it wasn't

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.064
an indication

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:31.998
of where he thinks

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.500
this investigation is going.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:33.733
Of course,

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.435
we don't ultimately know what Jack

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:36.503
Schmidt, the special counsel

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:37.404
who's investigating

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:39.372
this, is going to decide

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:40.673
I think it's easy for you all to say,

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:40.840
you know,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.809
there are a lot of Trump attorneys.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:43.576
Which one is this?

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:44.411
This is partly to me.

00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:46.613
He was the one who helped organize

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:47.847
those additional searches

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:48.248
that they did

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:49.115
for more documents

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:50.283
and other Trump properties

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:51.518
where they went and looked for them.

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:52.452
He testified

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:53.453
before the grand jury

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:55.555
that's investigating the documents

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:56.423
back in December.

00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:57.557
He was there for several hours.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:58.491
And one key thing

00:00:58.491 --> 00:00:59.526
he said in March,

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:01.194
it was reported that he had gone.

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:01.995
He said they kept trying

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:03.329
to ask him about conversations

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.431
he had with Trump, clearly

00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:06.266
trying to get to Trump's mindset.

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:07.067
He's an attorney.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.802
He I think he said that at the time.

00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:11.071
He did not speak to them about that.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:12.405
One thing I will note,

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.339
though, is this comes

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:14.808
as he had defended

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:16.876
the way that Trump had handled

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.812
the documents, made this comment

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:22.582
in a case

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.150
where they have to prove willful

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.618
retention of documents.

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:26.753
What the facts actually

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:29.055
show is a persistent pattern

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:31.591
of willful return of marked documents

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.660
whenever they were found.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.627
And so I went through all

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:36.129
that with the jury.

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:38.131
So that was his grand jury testimony.

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:38.865
The other thing that I think

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:40.233
is important here,

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.001
there is always infighting

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.269
in almost every legal team.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:44.237
Trump has had,

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:45.405
I think, dating back to

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:46.773
when he entered the White House,

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:47.107
as long as

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:48.908
we've been covering his legal teams

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:50.443
There has also been that in

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.445
this legal team, not just on documents,

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:54.380
but there's so many different attorneys.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.648
Joe Tacopina is the one

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.250
that you've seen representing Trump

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:57.984
in the aging Carol

00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.452
case here in New York where he was found

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.688
civilly liable for sexual abuse.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:04.691
I had Tim Pawlenty on.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:06.359
I was interviewing him a few weeks ago,

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.061
maybe two months ago or so, and we asked

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:08.461
whether or not

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.063
he thought Tacopina was the right person

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:12.332
to take that case to trial.

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:13.666
He had a really telling answer.

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:15.034
This is what he said.

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:18.938
As to who's going to try the case.

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:21.341
I know that Joe has certain

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:22.842
potential conflict issues

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.744
given his prior contacts

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.446
with Stormy Daniels so,

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:26.679
you know,

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.381
who is the right attorney to take a trial

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:29.015
is something that

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:31.651
the client will have to decide

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:35.288
Yeah, that didn't go over well.

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:37.757
I think the other thing to consider here

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:37.957
is that

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:39.292
Trump has been very unhappy

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:40.493
with all the investigations

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:41.761
into him privately

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:43.530
especially the documents investigation,

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.197
because he's paid

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:45.899
a lot of attorneys, a lot of money.

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:47.500
And he's been asking people privately,

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.101
privately, you know,

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:49.569
why is this still going on?

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:50.803
Why hasn't had gone away yet?

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:52.405
Obviously, it's a very complicated case.

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:53.740
We don't know where it will end up.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.308
But it is notable that Tim Pawlenty

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:56.442
is leaving his legal team.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.444
He was on that case and testified

00:02:58.444 --> 00:02:59.379
before the grand jury.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:02:59.812
Let's talk

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:01.681
more about this news that Caitlyn broke

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:02.849
with our political director,

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:03.816
David Chalian,

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.118
also the host of the CNN

00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:06.920
Political Briefing podcast.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:07.987
What's your take, David?

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:09.522
Well, first of

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:09.722
all, it's

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:10.890
always an awkward thing

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.860
when you are the legal representation

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.995
and also a witness in the case

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:17.463
that has to testify

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:19.866
that that creates problems.

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:20.767
From the get go there.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:23.002
But as Caitlin noted,

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:24.337
infighting in Trump's

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.172
legal circles is nothing new here.

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:27.307
So whenever we see

00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:27.707
a Trump

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.042
legal team formed,

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.176
we can pretty much assume

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:31.578
that's not going to be

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:32.679
the team in place

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:35.415
when the case comes to resolution here.

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:37.951
You know, this classified documents case,

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:39.886
especially the potential for

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:41.888
and obstruction of justice charge here

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.624
is one of the most serious investigations

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.292
that the former president is facing.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.361
So it's not a great day

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.463
when you have to shake up your legal team

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.599
for the client

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:53.499
if indeed it is

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:54.434
as serious as a case

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:55.902
as we believe this to be.

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.470
Yeah, we'll see what happens there.

